      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "From a physiological standpoint, a stalled squat progress can be attributed to various factors, but deloading or changing volume/intensity is a common solution. Decreasing the volume or intensity of the squat stimuli can allow for a micro-tear reaction, revisiting the adaptive mechanisms that drove initial gains, and potentially reigniting hypertrophic adaptations. Reducing volume could involve decreasing sets or reps, while intensity might mean reducing weight or performance- emphasis on technique. This method gives the muscles time to recover and rebuild, making the squat stimulus more potent when the client returns to training.",
